{
  "rules": [
    "Uptrend underestimation: when the last 5–10 days show a clear rebound/acceleration (e.g., +2 to +4 EUR from a local low), the model systematically under-forecasts the next 2–4 weeks. Apply a positive drift uplift that grows with horizon (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors are modest in the first ~7–10 days, but from ~day 12 onward the model is consistently too low versus realized prices. Use an increasing additive correction with lead time (e.g., +0.5 to +1 EUR in days 1–10, +2 to +4 EUR in days 11–20, +4 to +7 EUR in days 21–30, scaled to recent momentum).",
    "Lag/slow reaction to regime shift: after a drawdown followed by a sharp recovery (V-shape), the model mean-reverts too aggressively and fails to follow-through. Detect V-shapes (recent low within last ~10 days + strong rebound) and reduce mean-reversion strength; shift the forecast path upward and slightly steeper.",
    "Missed late-horizon breakout risk: the model rarely projects sustained moves into the upper tail (e.g., >68–70) even when realized does. If price crosses above recent 20–30d median by >~2 EUR and momentum is positive, cap/ceiling assumptions should be relaxed and an extra convex uplift added to days 15–30.",
    "Over-smoothing around spikes: the model produces a relatively flat path while realized outcomes trend strongly. When realized volatility in the last ~10 days is elevated (large daily ranges), increase forecast variance and allow trend continuation rather than damping; implement a trend-following adjustment proportional to recent slope."
  ]
}
